From: aiueo52 <aiueo52@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:01:49 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 062/105] fix(adaptive): drop post-switch stale accept samples
 (ping-pong)

First working run of --speculative-adaptive on Qwen3.8-Flash-Next made 489
state switches in 90 s -- every single decision interval flipped, and
code-edit acceptance collapsed to 5.57 (fixed W16: 9.85).

Verify results reach the controller one or more batches after the decision
that produced them: the scheduler feeds on_verify_complete_cpu off the
overlap queue, and a decided switch is only applied at the next batch
boundary. So the first samples after a 15 -> 3 switch still carry steps=15
accept lengths, and with ema_alpha=0.2 one leftover reading of ~9 accepted
drafts drags a freshly seeded steps=3 EMA (2.0) to ~3.4 -- straight back
over the step-up threshold.

Such a sample is identifiable: an accept count larger than the current
chain cannot have been produced by it. Drop those; the surviving samples
are all measurable at the live step count.

Also raises the shipped config's step-up threshold to 2.5 accepted drafts
(measured @steps=3: code-edit 2.81, agent-loop 2.24, prose-en 1.54).

The unit tests now model the pipeline lag explicitly and bound the switch
count at <= 4 per 300 batches per workload.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
